arXiv:2509.11773v1 [cs.CL] 15 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">An Agentic Toolkit for Adaptive Information Extraction 
<br class="ltx_break">from Regulatory Documents</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Gaye Colakoglu
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Zurich University of Applied Sciences, Switzerland
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>NEC Laboratories Europe, Heidelberg, Germany
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">colgay01@students.zhaw.ch</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Gürkan Solmaz
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>NEC Laboratories Europe, Heidelberg, Germany
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">guerkan.solmaz@neclab.eu</span></a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jonathan Fürst
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Zurich University of Applied Sciences, Switzerland
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">jonathan.fuerst@zhaw.ch</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Declaration of Performance (DoP) documents, mandated by EU regulation, certify the performance of construction products. While some of their content is standardized, DoPs vary widely in layout, language, schema, and format, posing challenges for automated key-value pair extraction (KVP) and question answering (QA). Existing static or LLM-only IE pipelines often hallucinate and fail to adapt to this structural diversity.
Our domain-specific, stateful agentic system addresses these challenges through a planner-executor-responder architecture. The system infers user intent, detects document modality, and orchestrates tools dynamically for robust, traceable reasoning while avoiding tool misuse or execution loops.
Evaluation on a curated DoP dataset demonstrates improved robustness across formats and languages, offering a scalable solution for structured data extraction in regulated workflows.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">DoP documents are mandated under the EU Construction Products Regulation (CPR) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">European Commission (2014)</a></cite>, certify that construction products meet defined performance criteria. Though the CPR specifies required content, real-world DoPs vary widely in format: they may be scanned or digital, structured or semi-structured, and multilingual (e.g., German or English). Layouts differ by manufacturer, with key information dispersed across tables, free-form text, and hybrid formats.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Automating KVP extraction and QA from DoPs helps reduce manual work and improve digital compliance processes. But existing pipelines often struggle with noisy scans, inconsistent layouts, and multiple languages <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al. (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sarkhel and Nandi (2021)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Perot et al. (2023)</a></cite>. Unlike prompt- or vision-only systems, agentic systems can track decisions, recover from errors, and adapt tools dynamically, making them a better fit for complex, real-world documents.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.11773v1/Figures/system-workflow.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/392;" width="476" height="392" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>User-agent workflow with internal planner, executor, and responder coordination.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a domain-specific agentic system for DoP documents that infers user intent (KVP or QA), detects modality (scanned or digital), and selects tools via a planner-executor-responder loop( <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ An Agentic Toolkit for Adaptive Information Extraction from Regulatory Documents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Inspired by tool-augmented agents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schick et al. (2023)</a></cite>, modular planners <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2023b)</a></cite>, and structured frameworks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2024)</a></cite>, it maintains state and adapts to user goals and document structure for improved robustness.
Further, we release a manually annotated DoP dataset with human-labeled KVP and QA annotations. Experiments show our agent outperforms GPT-4o and vision-based pipelines, especially on diverse layouts and languages.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, our main contributions are:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose an agentic system that adapts to user intent and document modality for KVP and QA tasks on DoP documents.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We release a to our knowledge first, multilingual DoP dataset with human-labeled KVP and QA annotation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We benchmark against GPT-4o and vision-based baselines, demonstrating improved generalization across layout and language variations.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Lastly, we share our code and annotated DoP dataset for usage and reproduction by the research community as part of the supplementary material.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Visually Rich Document Understanding.</span>
VRDU supports tasks such as invoice processing, identity verification, and form parsing. Advances have been driven by layout-aware models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2021)</a></cite> and multimodal datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jaume et al. (2019)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al. (2019)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2019)</a></cite> that combine text, layout, and visual features for structured extraction. However, these systems are typically trained on clean, monolingual, and semantically regular documents. In contrast, DoP documents exhibit high variability in layout, quality, and language, making them underrepresented and challenging for standard VRDU pipelines.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt-Based and End-to-End Extraction.</span>
Prompt-based methods extend document understanding by framing extraction as question answering <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al. (2023)</a></cite>, incorporating layout structure <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al. (2023)</a></cite>, or directly decoding fields from images <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al. (2021)</a></cite>. While these approaches reduce reliance on rigid schemas, they often assume consistently formatted, high-quality input.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tool-Augmented Reasoning and Modular Agents.</span>
Recent work enhances LLMs with tool use for modular and adaptive reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schick et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang and Duan (2024)</a></cite>, but most document pipelines remain static, relying on fixed OCR and parsing sequences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luo et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Perot et al. (2023)</a></cite>. More flexible designs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Neupane et al. (2025)</a></cite> introduce partial modularity (e.g., knowledge guidance or layout-aware prompts) but lack runtime planning or tool orchestration. Agentic systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Patil et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qin et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al. (2023)</a></cite> decouple planning, tool use, and response generation via dynamic API calls or subprograms. While benchmarks such as AgentBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2023b)</a></cite> evaluate such agents in interactive environments, they do not address structured, document-grounded tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Planning, Feedback, and Memory in Agents.</span>
Surveys of LLM agents highlight mechanisms such as task decomposition, plan revision, and tool routing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Masterman et al. (2024)</a></cite>. Iterative refinement methods implement feedback loops for recovery <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shinn et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Madaan et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. (2023a)</a></cite>, which our agent mirrors via a planning-execution-feedback cycle to handle OCR or parsing failures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Structured planning frameworks support modular reasoning and collaborative workflows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2024)</a></cite>, while code-trained LLMs offer stronger schema-aware planning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2024)</a></cite>, motivating our choice of a structured planner. Long-term memory mechanisms-such as scratchpads or persistent state <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2024b)</a></cite>-are integrated in our <span id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">AgentState</span> to track modality, task intent, and tool outcomes. Retrieval-augmented planners <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ruan et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kong et al. (2023)</a></cite> also inform our approach to runtime tool selection across diverse document types.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Evaluation Results</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Results for the KVP Task</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we evaluate model performance on the KVP extraction using the 12 primary keys defined in Section <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>. Two of these keys contain nested values; that is, they represent additional layers of KVP with varying depth and no fixed structure. For this initial evaluation, we treat the values of all keys-including the nested ones (e.g., <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">value_de</span> in Listing <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>) as flat strings. A more detailed evaluation of nested structures is provided in Appendix <a href="#A1.SS3.SSS5" title="A.3.5 Aggregation of Evaluation Metrics for Nested-KVP Task ‣ A.3 Agentic System Evaluation Related Details ‣ Appendix A Appendix ‣ An Agentic Toolkit for Adaptive Information Extraction from Regulatory Documents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The evaluation results are organized according to the metric categories introduced in Section <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>, namely JSON Validity, Key Match Ratio, and Value Similarity.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">JSON Validity.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin the analysis by assessing JSON validity, which serves as a baseline requirement for all model outputs: generating a well-formed JSON object that conforms to the target key hierarchy. Both the agent and baseline models are prompted to return outputs in valid JSON.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ JSON Validity. ‣ 3.1 Results for the KVP Task ‣ 3 Evaluation Results ‣ An Agentic Toolkit for Adaptive Information Extraction from Regulatory Documents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, both the agent and GPT-4o consistently produce 100% valid JSON outputs across all configurations, indicating strong formatting reliability regardless of prompting strategy or user language.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>JSON validity rates for fixed-schema and open-schema KVP across agent and baseline models, two prompting strategies (T: task only, T+S: task plus schema), and two user languages (en, de).</figcaption>
<div id="S3.T1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:88.8pt;vertical-align:-41.4pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(28.0pt,-7.2pt) scale(1.1937419401675,1.1937419401675) ;">
<table id="S3.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S3.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_border_tt" rowspan="2"></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" colspan="2"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT-4o</span>  </td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" colspan="2"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT-4o-V</span>  </td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Agent</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">T</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">T+S</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">T</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">T+S</td>
