<td id="S3.T1.2.1.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="--ltx-bg-color:#BFBFBF;"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.6.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#BFBFBF;">1.000</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.6.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.818</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.6.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.982</td>
<td id="S3.T1.2.1.6.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="--ltx-bg-color:#BFBFBF;"><span id="S3.T1.2.1.6.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#BFBFBF;">1.000</span></td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">GPT-4o-V, however, shows reduced robustness, particularly under minimal guidance (T). This is likely due to the added complexity of visually parsing the document and composing structured JSON without direct access to clean text, making it more error-prone in syntax-sensitive tasks. The addition of schema and key information (T+S) improves its performance across both English and German prompts, suggesting that the model relies more heavily on explicit structural cues. This improvement is more pronounced with English prompts, where JSON validity increases from 0.885 to 0.980, compared to a smaller gain for German prompts (from 0.904 to 0.970). One possible explanation is the mismatch between the English prompt and the predominantly German document content (96%), which may cause ambiguity when the prompt lacks structural guidance. By contrast, German prompts naturally align with the document language and help the model infer structure more reliably. When schema information is provided, it compensates for the language mismatch, leading to a larger relative gain for English prompts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Key Match Ratio.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We next evaluate whether model outputs include the expected top-level keys, as defined by the schema provided in the prompt. This key-level evaluation is applied only to outputs that are valid JSON, since structured parsing is required. For malformed outputs, a KeyMatchRatio of zero is assigned to ensure consistent averaging across documents and to prevent inflated scores. Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Key Match Ratio. ‣ 3.1 Results for the KVP Task ‣ 3 Evaluation Results ‣ An Agentic Toolkit for Adaptive Information Extraction from Regulatory Documents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> reports KeyMatchRatio scores across two user languages (English and German), comparing GPT-4o, GPT-4o-V, and the agent. Each system is evaluated under two prompting conditions: T and T+S.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Both the agent and GPT-4o achieve full key coverage when provided with explicit schema guidance (T+S), demonstrating their ability to reliably reproduce the expected top-level structure. GPT-4o-V, in contrast, performs poorly under minimal guidance (T), particularly in English (0.003), but improves substantially with the addition of schema cues-highlighting its stronger dependence on explicit structural hints. The agent matches GPT-4o’s performance across both languages. Importantly, while GPT-4o requires carefully tailored prompts for each task and language, our agent encapsulates this complexity internally. This design enables consistent and robust execution across multilingual DoP documents and task types, while reducing the burden on end users.</p>
</div>
