</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The results in Table <a href="#S3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Value Similarity. ‣ 3.1 Results for the KVP Task ‣ 3 Evaluation Results ‣ An Agentic Toolkit for Adaptive Information Extraction from Regulatory Documents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> present a more nuanced view of model performance at the value level, which is often the most error-prone stage in real-world KVP extraction. While all systems benefit from schema guidance (T+S), the agent consistently outperforms both GPT-4o and GPT-4o-V across all three metrics, even though it uses the same underlying foundation model (GPT-4o). For example, compared to GPT-4o (T+S), the agent improves Exact Match by 72.4% (en) and 1.4% (de), and ROUGE by 56.9% (en) and 2.6% (de). BLEU gains are 69.4% (en) and 5.3% (de). These improvements indicate that the agent’s internal reasoning, schema enforcement, and postprocessing meaningfully enhance output quality beyond prompt engineering alone.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The agent delivers near-identical results across English and German, with  0.2% variation on average across all metrics. In contrast, GPT-4o and GPT-4o-V vary by up to 70%, depending on the language and metric. This cross-lingual stability is critical for robust DoP document processing, where multilingual inputs are common. While GPT-4o (T+S) performs competitively, particularly on German prompts, likely due to the predominance of German text in the dataset, it still depends on manually crafted, task- and language-specific prompts. The agent encapsulates this complexity, offering more consistent and language-agnostic outputs with minimal user input.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Results for the QA Task</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the QA capabilities of the agentic system using three complementary metrics that capture both surface-level and semantic fidelity: Exact Match, BLEU, and ROUGE. As in the KVP evaluation, we report results from two complementary perspectives: the full QA dataset (<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">All QA Data</span>), which includes questions derived from both flat and nested fields, and a focused subset targeting only questions derived from nested key-value structures (<span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Nested QA Data</span>). This allows us to assess performance under different structural complexities.
Table <a href="#S3.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 3.2 Results for the QA Task ‣ 3 Evaluation Results ‣ An Agentic Toolkit for Adaptive Information Extraction from Regulatory Documents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> presents a comparative analysis of the agentic system and two baseline models, showing their performance across both evaluation scopes and user languages (English and German).</p>
</div>
