<td id="S3.T4.2.1.9.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="--ltx-bg-color:#BFBFBF;"><span id="S3.T4.2.1.9.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#BFBFBF;">0.645</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T4.2.1.10" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.10.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_align_left">GPT-4o</td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.10.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.577</td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.10.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.642</td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.10.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.540</td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.10.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.636</td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T4.2.1.11" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.11.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_l ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">GPT-4o-V</td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.11.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.526</td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.11.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.640</td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.11.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.337</td>
<td id="S3.T4.2.1.11.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.406</td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The agent performs consistently better on German queries across both QA types. For flat questions, it achieves an Exact Match of 0.494 and ROUGE of 0.712 in German, compared to 0.323 and 0.633 in English. BLEU also favors German, with a 13.5-point margin (0.474 vs. 0.339), likely due to stronger alignment between the user and document language and more consistent phrasing in the German data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Nested QA, which targets deeper semantic structures, reveals increased difficulty. While Exact Match slightly improves for English (from 0.323 to 0.370), BLEU and ROUGE scores decline. In German, all three metrics drop when moving from flat to nested QA, indicating that the agent struggles to generate fluent or complete answers in more hierarchical contexts. The BLEU decline is especially pronounced in English (-10.5 points), reflecting lexical variation or partial generations in response to complex structures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">These results suggest that the system is effective at handling structured declarations in German but still faces challenges in multilingual generalization and semantic depth. To further contextualize these findings, we are in the process of running baseline evaluations using GPT-4o and GPT-4o-Vision models for the QA task-mirroring the setup used for KVP extraction. This comparison will help quantify the benefits of agentic reasoning and assess the added value of vision-enhanced models for layout-sensitive or semantically complex inputs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Runtime, Cost and Token Comparison</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We further compare the computational efficiency of the agentic system against both baseline models in terms of token usage, total cost, and runtime. Table <a href="#S3.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 3.3 Runtime, Cost and Token Comparison ‣ 3 Evaluation Results ‣ An Agentic Toolkit for Adaptive Information Extraction from Regulatory Documents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> summarizes these metrics across the KVP and QA tasks. Input and output token counts are derived from the prompt and completion segments, respectively, with visual or textual context included in input estimates. The cost is computed based on the GPT-4o pricing, and the run time reflects the total wall clock time per tasks. Although the agentic system provides performance benefits, the cost in terms of runtime and token usage is higher, which creates an open research problem.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T5" class="ltx_table">
