</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper introduces an agentic system for IE from Declaration of Performance (DoP) documents, with a focus on handling both flat and nested key-value structures in English and German. Our agent consistently generates 100% valid JSON and significantly outperforms GPT-4 baselines in matching nested keys. The agent’s primary strength is its cross-lingual stability, with performance varying less than 1% between languages, whereas baselines fluctuate by up to 18%.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Limitations</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While the agent outperforms current baselines (on KVP task), several avenues for enhancement remain, particularly within the DoP domain. Current evaluation metrics, especially for KeyMatchRatio, are overly strict and penalize semantically valid outputs that differ in structure or lexical form-such as merged fields (“240 x 248”) or orthographic variants (“Lobnitz” vs. “Löbnitz”). Introducing fuzzy or schema-aware matching would enable more accurate evaluation, especially for nested structures.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Further, while the agent currently follows predefined routing paths based on document type and language, etc., it already incorporates error-aware fallback and tool usage history to adapt when failures occur. Future work could enhance this adaptability by integrating confidence-aware self-reflection and more dynamic planning to proactively prevent failures and optimize tool selection in ambiguous cases. To increase value accuracy for nested keys, selective use of visual cues-such as layout, table structure, or text positioning, helps resolve ambiguities that pure language models struggle with. A hybrid strategy that preserves language model interpretability while leveraging structural information could offer a practical balance.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, the logic used for flattening nested keys-applied during both QA dataset generation and evaluation of nested key-value structures-should be improved to allow greater flexibility. The current strict flattening approach is a primary reason for the lower scores observed on nested structures. These improvements, alongside refined evaluation protocols, will strengthen the agent’s reliability and semantic precision for structured extraction in multilingual DoP documents.</p>
</div>
</section>
